Title: 'Potestates fractionum ad Circuli Quadraturam una cum residuis', i.e. powers of fractions for the quadrature of the circle, together with remainders. The page is a table of successive powers 1/n^k, worked out to about 60 decimal places by repeated long division. It supplies the terms of the Gregory series arccot n = Σ_k (-1)^k /((2k+1) n^(2k+1)), which feeds an arctangent (Machin-type) formula for π.

Section I, n = 307. Let N = 10^60. Line 1 is L1 = floor(N/307) = 3257328990228013029315960912052117263843648208469055374592. The 1/307 = 0.0032573289... is written without its leading zeros and grouped in tens. The parenthesis '(256' at the top is the remainder 10^60 − 307·L1 = 256.

For each later line, L_{k+1} = floor(L_k/307), and r_k = L_k − 307·L_{k+1} is the 'residuum' written at the right margin of line k. The residues are therefore offset by half a line: (256) belongs to producing line 1, (132) to dividing line 1 to get line 2, and so on. The last '(2' comes from 2 = 0·307 + 2. My own spot-checks confirm the scheme:
- 307·L2 + 132 ends …9055374592 (= L1);
- 307·L4 + 268 ends …1556823471 (= L3);
- 307·L8 + 121 ends …6274855960 (= L7);
- 307·58897127 + 98 = 18081418178;
- 191847 = 307·624 + 279;
- 624 = 2·307 + 10.

Since floor(floor(a/b)/c) = floor(a/(bc)), L_k = floor(10^60/307^k) = the first 60 decimal places of 307^(-k). The table stops at k = 24, where 10^60/307^24 ≈ 2.04. All powers are computed because Gauss divides by 307 once per step. Only the odd ones, L1, L3, …, L23, are used on the previous page: there they are divided by 1, 3, 5, …, 23, summed with alternating signs, and give arccot 307 ≈ 0.0032573174700585734… The previous page's heading 'Acotg 307 = −3(2)+3(5)+(13)+(29)' records how 307+i factors into Gaussian primes. This rests on 307²+1 = 2·5³·13·29, Gauss's standard technique for finding arctangent relations.

Section II, n = 882 (note 882²+1 = 5²·29²·37). The same procedure applied to 1/882, carried only to the 12th power. There are no marginal residues except '(316' on line 1, which is 10^60 mod 882. The digit groups are aligned in fixed absolute positions: each line moves about 3 digits to the right, since log10 882 ≈ 2.945. The leading-group lengths 8, 5, 2, 9, 6, 3, 10, 7, 4, 1, 8, 5 confirm this. Values checked against 1/882^k:
- 1/882 = 0.0011337868 4807256235 …;
- 1/882² = 1.2854726…e-6;
- 1/882³ = 1.4574519…e-9;
- 1/882⁶ = 2.124166…e-18;
- 1/882⁷ = 2.40835167…e-21;
- 1/882¹² = 4.51208…e-36.
The last group of each line in II is written only partly and is heavily overwritten in lines 8–12. The section looks unfinished: about 20 powers would be needed for 60 places.

Marginal numbers 385791 and 8827: their purpose is unclear. Note 8827 = 7·13·97 and 385791 = 3·7·18371.
